  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nLingering low level moisture will be allowing for plenty of\nclouds to start the day today. Some breaks are expected by this\nafternoon. Latest 3km HRRR suggest temperatures may be slightly\nlower than what was suggested by the NBM, but temps will still\nprobably top out around or slightly above normal, with valley\nhighs this afternoon in the mid to upper 60s. \n\nA wave of low pressure along a frontal boundary will be passing\nby to the north for tonight into Tuesday. This will allow for \nsome showers, especially for northern areas. Highest POPs look\nto be for during the day on Tuesday across the Adirondacks,\nalthough total amounts will generally be under an inch. The\n50% or greater probability of 1\" or more is limited to far\nnorthern Herkimer and Hamilton Counties, but much lower\nprobabilities to the south. Considering the limited precip and\nmanageable rainfall rates, no flooding is anticipated through \nthe mid-week.\n\nThere could be a few rumbles of thunder on Tuesday across the\nregion, but instability is fairly limited. Mean SBCAPE off the\nNBM is generally under 200 J/jg across our area, with the higher\nvalues well west of the region over western New York and across\nwestern and central Pennsylvania. ML/AI guidance suggest any\npotential for damaging wind gusts should stay well of the\nregion, which makes sense considering the limited instability in\nplace. Will keep mention of slight chc for thunder in place, but\nany storms storms should remain west of the area. In addition, another\nmild day is expected for Tuesday, with highs once again well \ninto the 60s. Some southern areas may reach into the low 70s, \ndepending on if enough sunshine occurs.\n\nIt looks to stay mild into Wednesday as well, but a cold front\nshould be crossing during the morning hours on Wednesday. This\nwill allow temps to cool slightly for Thursday-Friday, but the\nreal chilly air doesn't arrive until the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAnother frontal boundary looks to cross the region for Friday\ninto Friday night. A strong upper level trough will be passing\nover the Northeast for the weekend, with 850 hpa temps dropping\ndown to around -8 to -10 C according to the 00z GEFS, which is \nwell below normal for mid October. 00z GEFS 500 hpa heights are\nabout 2-3 STD below normal as well. Although moisture will be\nlimited behind the cold front, some lake enhanced and upslope\nprecip may occur behind the front, allowing for some snow\nshowers for western, northern and high terrain areas. At this\npoint, its hard to stay how much (if at all) any accumulation\nwould occur, but the first flakes of the season are a\npossibility of the higher terrain areas with the expected chilly\nair in place. In addition, a strong pressure gradient will\nresult in breezy to windy conditions, making it feel even colder\nthrough the weekend. Although the growing season has ended for \nthe entire region, a widespread freeze is likely Saturday night \nfor the entire area as well.",
